    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The seven-transmembrane oncoprotein Smoothened (Smo) is a key activator in the Hedgehog (Hh) signaling pathway, and thus a driver of many processes in tissue patterning and postembryonic stem cell regulation. In normal pathway activity, Smo is repressed by the tumor suppressor protein Patched (Ptch), and this repression is relieved when the Hh morphogen binds to and inactivates Ptch. However, the mechanism of Ptch action on Smo is poorly understood. In this study, the approaches of cyclopamine pocket mutagenesis and functional phylogenetic comparisons of five Smo orthologs (urchin, tunicate, lamprey, skate, and coelacanth) were used to identify key determinants of the regulatory interaction between Ptch and Smo. Mouse-lamprey Smo chimeras provided evidence for a role of the cysteine-rich domain in restraining signaling activity, and suggested a two-site model of Ptch regulation. To address the difficulty of measuring pathway activity of basal orthologs in vertebrate cells, a new FRET assay was developed that provides a more direct readout of Smo activity state. This assay, based on Smo coupling to a G protein alpha subunit, has broad applications in the Hedgehog field. The regulatory mechanism of Smo is an open question in developmental and oncogenic signaling, and a better understanding of the Ptch-Smo interaction will lay the groundwork for more effective cancer therapies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
